cartridge "PLANET RAIDER/GAZETTE/1990"

' PLANET RAIDER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Planet Raider", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, March
' 1990 (Commodore 64 machine language, started by SYS2064; no author is
' named on the disk). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's, read from its
' machine code (orig/praider.dis; jsc64 runs in orig/ showed the screen but
' not the play: see README.md), rebuilt for the ColecoVision with smooth
' sprites, tile caverns and sound.
'
' The game: fly a raider ship down the shaft of an enemy planet. Gravity
' pulls it down; turn and thrust to steer, shoot the gun bases on the
' girders, reach the glowing core at the bottom of the shaft to complete
' the level. Touching rock, a girder or a base, or being hit by a base's
' shot, costs a ship. Thrust and the shield burn fuel; a ship out of fuel
' can only fall. TIME runs down while you fly: what is left is the time
' bonus (20 points a unit); at 0 there is NO TIME BONUS.
'
' From the original (orig/praider.dis): the ship turning in 16 headings
' (sprite pointers $D0-$DF), thrust along the heading and a constant pull
' (gravity, $0E41), a shot from the nose, a shield on its own control that
' burns fuel ($09D9, $0A12), gun bases on the cavern that fire at random
' moments ($0B1F), five ships, FUEL refilled for each new ship ($0D3A),
' the status line SCORE / ships / FUEL / TIME, the level ending when the
' ship reaches the bottom of the cavern ($08EB), "TIME BONUS = 20 x time"
' or "NO TIME BONUS", "LEVEL X COMPLETE", and after every eighth level
' "ALL ENEMY BASES DESTROYED! 2 EXTRA SHIPS!" when all 24 bases of those
' eight levels were shot ($BF, $0D9F); bases fire more often as the levels
' go on ($BE); after a lost ship the level starts again from the top with
' the destroyed bases still gone ($0D2E); "GAME OVER".
'
' Liberties: the C64's eight caverns were line drawings packed in its own
' format ($189F...); here eight new caverns are built from tiles (art.py),
' 60 rows deep, scrolled a row at a time; the C64's fuel tanks picked up
' with the shield are fuel pods on girders (500 fuel and 100 points each,
' collected by the shield within 12 pixels); a base is worth 300; TIME
' starts at 99 for each level and drops one unit every two seconds (as the
' C64's every 128 jiffies); gravity always points down (the C64 reversed
' it on levels 9-16; the shafts here only go down); the joystick only:
' left/right turn, up thrusts, down raises the shield, either button fires
' (the C64 used keys and the joystick together); enemy shots are aimed at
' the ship with a little scatter, at one pixel a frame.
'
' Controls: controller 1. Stick left/right turns, up thrusts, down holds
' the shield (also the fuel tractor), either button fires. On the title
' up/down and the button choose START, HOW TO PLAY or CREDITS.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29; tiles from code 128; the frame loop is
' protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off after a wait,
' display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after; the title
' tune is played once. Own xorshift random numbers.

